rtment of Radiation Oncology, Research Institute GROW, Maastricht University, Maastricht, Netherlands."}]}}, {"fullName": "Leijenaar RT", "lastName": "Leijenaar", "firstName": "Ralph Th", "initials": "RT", "authorId": {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0001-8642-9657"}, "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Department of Radiation Oncology, Research Institute GROW, Maastricht University, Maastricht, Netherlands."}]}}, {"fullName": "Haibe-Kains B", "lastName": "Haibe-Kains", "firstName": "Benjamin", "initials": "B", "authorId": {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-7684-0079"}, "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Princess Margaret Cancer Centre, University Health Network, University of Toronto, Toronto, Canada."}, {"affiliation": "Medical Biophysics Department, University of Toronto, Toronto, Canada."}]}}, {"fullName": "Lambin P", "lastName": "Lambin", "firstName": "Philippe", "initials": "P", "authorId": {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0001-7961-0191"}, "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Department of Radiation Oncology, Research Institute GROW, Maastricht University, Maastricht, Netherlands."}]}}, {"fullName": "Gillies RJ", "lastName": "Gillies", "firstName": "Robert J", "initials": "RJ", "authorId": {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-8888-7747"}, "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Department of Cancer Imaging and Metabolism, H. Lee Moffitt Cancer Center and Research Institute, Tampa, United States."}]}}, {"fullName": "Aerts HJ", "lastName": "Aerts", "firstName": "Hugo Jwl", "initials": "HJ", "authorId": {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-2122-2003"}, "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Department of Radiation Oncology, Dana-Farber Cancer Institute, Brigham and Women's Hospital, Harvard Medical School, Boston, United States."}, {"affiliation": "Department of Biostatistics and Computational Biology, Dana-Farber Cancer Institute, Boston, United States."}, {"affiliation": "Department of Radiology, Brigham and Women's Hospital, Harvard Medical School, Boston, United States."}]}}]}, "authorIdList": {"authorId": [{"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0001-7961-0191"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0001-8642-9657"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-2122-2003"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-2140-814X"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-5778-5342"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-7684-0079"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-8888-7747"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0003-4918-6902"}]}, "dataLinksTagsList": {"dataLinkstag": ["altmetrics", "supporting_data", "related_data"]}, "journalInfo": {"volume": "6", "journalIssueId": 2572776, "dateOfPublication": "2017 Jul", "monthOfPublication": 7, "yearOfPublication": 2017, "journal": {"medlineAbbreviation": "Elife", "title": "eLife", "essn": "2050-084X", "issn": "2050-084X", "nlmid": "101579614", "isoabbreviation": "Elife"}, "printPublicationDate": "2017-07-01"}, "pubYear": "2017", "pageInfo": "e23421", "abstractText": "Medical imaging can visualize characteristics of human cancer noninvasively. Radiomics is an emerging field that translates these medical images into quantitative data to enable phenotypic profiling of tumors. While radiomics has been associated with several clinical endpoints, the complex relationships of radiomics, clinical factors, and tumor biology are largely unknown. To this end, we analyzed two independent cohorts of respectively 262 North American and 89 European patients with lung cancer, and consistently identified previously undescribed associations between radiomic imaging features, molecular pathways, and clinical factors. In particular, we found a relationship between imaging features, immune response, inflammation, and survival, which was further validated by immunohistochemical staining. Moreover, a number of imaging features showed predictive value for specific pathways; for example, intra-tumor heterogeneity features predicted activity of RNA polymerase transcription (AUC = 0.62, p=0.03) and intensity dispersion was predictive of the autodegration pathway of a ubiquitin ligase (AUC = 0.69, p<i><</i>10<sup>-4</sup>). Finally, we observed that prognostic biomarkers performed highest when combining radiomic, genetic, and clinical information (CI = 0.73, p<10<sup>-9</sup>) indicating complementary value of these data. In conclusion, we demonstrate that radiomic approaches permit noninvasive assessment of both molecular and clinical characteristics of tumors, and therefore have the potential to advance clinical decision-making by systematically analyzing standard-of-care medical images.", "affiliation": "Department of Radiation Oncology, Dana-Farber Cancer Institute, Brigham and Women's Hospital, Harvard Medical School, Boston, United States.", "publicationStatus": "epublish", "language": "eng", "pubModel": "Electronic", "pubTypeList": {"pubType": ["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't", "research-article", "Journal Article", "Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]}, "grantsList": {"grant": [{"agency": "NCI NIH HHS", "acronym": "CA", "grantId": "P50 CA119997", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "Technologiestichting STW", "grantId": "10696 DuCA", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NCI NIH HHS", "acronym": "CA", "grantId": "P30 CA076292", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "Dutch Cancer Society", "grantId": "KWF UM 2009-4454", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NCI NIH HHS", "acronym": "CA", "grantId": "U01 CA143062", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "National Institutes of Health", "grantId": "NIH/NCI P50CA119997", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NCI NIH HHS", "acronym": "CA", "grantId": "U01 CA190234", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "Dutch Cancer Society", "grantId": "KWF MAC 2013-6425", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "National Institutes of Health", "grantId": "NIH-USA U24CA194354", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "National Institutes of Health", "grantId": "NIH/NCI U01CA143